1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.slovenska-biografija.si/oseba/sbi524204/
2 CONT
2 CONT Google Translation: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rudež Joseph, ethnographer, lord and patron, in Ribnica r. 30. aug. 1793 to the lord Anton (see article) and u. March 29, 1846. Visited 1807/8 6th class. gimn. in Lj., a second year philosopher graduated here. 1810, then spent one
2 CONC  year in Vienna under Kopitar's tutelage, ending the joint journey through Germany, from where they left until 21 October. 1811 returned: Kopitar to Vienna and R. to Ribnica. In 1812, R. married 16-year-old Joseph Koglova, daughte
2 CONC r of Bernard (SBL I, 489), and after her early death, Maria Sadnjek of Planina (1818). He purchased the Tolsti vrh manor from his father's extensive estate in 1827, and passed on to him: the Ribnica estate with the incorporated Br
2 CONC eg estate near Ribnica, the Dragomlje and Loke estates, the house in Lj. and Gorenji villages in the Ribnica lordship and some land in the Otočec lordship near Mokronog. L. 1842 tried to shake off the administration of the fishery
2 CONC  county farm, which only happened to him in 1846. - The gifted R. received the first initiative to procreate in a home accessible to all public workers. He was consciously and purposefully cultivated and guided by his personality 
2 CONC and interest by mentor Kopitar: he used to encourage him to look sober and to work life; encouraged writing, and with zealous acknowledgment of the already written prompts to continue; draw attention to contemporary relevant liter
2 CONC ature; introduced him (e.g., to J. Zupan in 1811; the latter became his father's friend) or mediated with men of similar aspirations (Schottky, Korytko?). More than accidental acquaintances were Ru, among others: Zois, Primic, Sta
2 CONC nko Vraz, Andrej Smole, Crobath, Prešeren. He systematically arranged a "Slavic library" in Ribniško Castle, where you found, among other things, Dobrovsky Slavin, Prolegomen, Kopitar Glagolitic Clozianus, KČ, Gajevo Ilirsko Danic
2 CONC a, Vrazovo Kolo, Vukov srb. b. songs, etc. He systematically arranged a "Slavic library" in Ribniško Castle, where you found, among other things, Dobrovsky Slavin, Prolegomen, Kopitar Glagolitic Clozianus, KČ, Gajevo Ilirsko Danic
2 CONC a, Vrazovo Kolo, Vukov srb. b. songs, etc. He systematically arranged a "Slavic library" in Ribniško Castle, where you found, among other things, Dobrovsky Slavin, Prolegomen, Kopitar Glagolitic Clozianus, KČ, Gajevo Ilirsko Danic
2 CONC a, Vrazovo Kolo, Vukov srb. b. songs, etc.
2 CONT
2 CONT As an ethnographer, R. published (anonymously) his first article Beschreibung einer Hochzeit, wie sie im Jahre 1811 auf dem Karst… gefeyert worden (published in UK 1811 by Kopitar). Despite his repeated warnings, he did not descri
2 CONC be his own marriage (August 30, 1812), but sent him linguistic peculiarities, which he also conveyed to Dobrovsky. He does not seem to have written reviews of Woltersdorf's book Illyrische Provinzen R., though Kopitar persistently
2 CONC  objected to it, and Kopitar's plan for an ethnographic description of the Illyrian nations was only partially fulfilled by his writings on the Kočevari. R's interest in Kočevje dates back to l. 1812; when (at least in 1819) he be
2 CONC gan to correspond with the young Prussian ethnographer Max Julius Schottky, he was able not only to answer many questions about the Kocevars, but to give him more material to use than Schottky had hoped for; on top of that he invi
2 CONC ted him to Ribnica so that Pruss could study the Kočevje issue in person. In Schottky's paper, R. published his own description of the Kochavars: Das Herzogthum Gottschee, besprochen durch Prof. dr. Richter und von Rudesch, Vorzei
2 CONC t u. Gegenwart, Posen 1823, 1st Band, 3rd Stück, S. 257-78: according to general geographical and historical data and etymological interpretation of the Kočevje R., it proves with local and family names, with a costume, customs an
2 CONC d with the authority of the church branch of Kočevar, that the Kočevars are descendants of the originally Slavic population and of its heavily mixed colonists. The Kočevski vocabulary, which differs from written German, the Kočevj
2 CONC e father-in-law, a list of family names and four folk ballads (Wie frühe steht auf Mogretitzle, Die Wochtel schluget and ünscher Falde, Der Woitzen hot geschossen, Mario! Von der Moierin). R. promised (oc. 277) that he would annou
2 CONC nce a tune to the last ballad, and Schottky publicly called upon him (oc. 271) to continue his research, since "no one is as vocal as he is"; but the leaf ceased to be published, and R. published nothing more about the substance.
2 CONT
2 CONT For over 100 years, R's careful and well-grounded findings served as a starting point and valuable material for all later ethnographers who were interested in the Kocevars, even if Ra was silenced (eg Radics), sometimes truncated 
2 CONC (sometimes by Schröer, Grothe) , when they were misled by their over-emphasized German orientation, or cited n. BC: Schroer (Ein Ausflug nach Gottschee, SBAW Wien 1868, LX. Bd., S. 165, I. Theil and LXV, 1870, Weitere Mittheilunge
2 CONC n über die Mundart von Gottschee), who admits that it contains "the most important" already R - the composition; Cook Fr. S. (South Slavic Pop Songs, I – IV, Zgb, 1878–81), which recounts - but spooked - one of R's published Kočev
2 CONC ski ballads; Josef Obergföll (Ueber die Herkunft der Gottscheer, Deutscher Kalender für Krain. Lb. 1888), who adheres to Schröer's implementations; dr. Due to its importance, Adolf Hauffen reprinted R's composition (LW 1893, Nr. 6
2 CONC 49, 650, Jan. 14 and 21) and also published in his book (Die deutsche Sprachinsel Gottschee, Quellen und Forschungen zur Geschichte, Literatur u. Sprache Oesterreichs und seiner Kronländer, III, Graz 1895, No. 101) returns to Schr
2 CONC öer's file and R's publications; anonymous writer of the article Der älteste Aufsatz über Gottchee (Gottscheer Zg 1928, Nos. 28, 31, 32), which reprints the earlier Hauffen file because it is the original, ie R-ev in Zeit u. Gegen
2 CONC wart, "almost inaccessible today"; he was greatly truncated by Grothe H. (Die deutsche Sprachinsel Gottschee and Slowenien, 1931); the last of Simonic's studies (Et 1934; the same, The Boatmen in the Light of Local and Glacial Nam
2 CONC es, GMS XVI, 1935, 61 to 81, 106–123) confirm the correctness of R's findings.
2 CONT
2 CONT R. continued his ethnographic and folkloristic work, but it is not known that he would have communicated anything else. Stanko Vraz, who was several times his guest in Ribnica, contributed material for a collection of folk songs. 
2 CONC R. received (15 August 19, 7 September, 4-11 November 1838) in Ribnica also E. Korytka and extensively supported him with Kočevje folk songs and ethnographic material (28 leaves in Korytko's rest ) for his intended monograph on Ko
2 CONC čevci. R. successfully forwarded 1842 to Prešeren and Crobath because of Korytko's residency for Vraz. R's language material also served Miklosic through Kopitar.
2 CONT
2 CONT As an economic expert and a member of KD in Lj. (1846) wrote R. Kurze Beschreibung der Landwirtschaft im Herrschaftsbezirk Reifnitz (Annalen der kk Landwirtschafts-Gesellschaft Jahrg. 1822–3, 1830, 59–76) and a short composition o
2 CONC f the Einige Notizen über den Maisbau and der Umgebung von Reifnitz 1843 II / 6, 80-1, as reported by N (1845, 142).
2 CONT
2 CONT R., »iuvenis slavicissimus«, »ardentis slavismi« (Kopitar), »ena najsimpatičnejših osebnosti slov. preporoda« (Kidrič), je ostal vse življenje zvest svojim mladostnim stremljenjem: velikopotezno in nesebično je podpiral vsako delo
2 CONC  za domačo stvar; prijateljem je bil vdan in ustrežljiv, do podložnikov razumevajoč in dober; kot etnograf in nabiratelj jezikovnih posebnosti doganjiv in temeljit, s posebnim darom za pisanje, vendar brez literarnih ambicij, osta
2 CONC jajoč skoro neopažen, kljub zelo laskavemu Kopitarjevemu in Dobrovskega priznanju. — Prim.: matrike v Ribnici; pismo ribniškega dekana Kidriču, 5. febr. 1931; poročilo ribniškega župnika 21. apr., 10., 16. maja, 6. junija 1952; re
2 CONC pertorij šenklavške poročne knjige; miscellanea za šolstvo v franc. dobi (ŠkALj); Instrumentenbuch N IX, 333–6; N IV, 142; Im 6. blauen Quatern E 9 (DAS); SBL I, 522, 523 (Korytko); Ist. I, II (kazalo: Rudež, Schottky); Kidrič, Zg
2 CONC od., kazalo; Marn XVI, 5, 6–7; Prijatelj, Profili 59, 60; Wurzbach XXXI, 251; Kopitar — Zoisu 3. dec. 1810, 7. jun. 1811, 10. marca 1812, 15. avg. 1812; Ms 455, 465, 468, 479, 577 št. 54, 973 VIII/B, 1008 št. 1, 2, 3 (vse v NUK); 
2 CONC Heinko, Carn. 1842, 370; N 1845, 142; 1846, 56; K. J. Schröer, Ein Ausflug nach Gottschee, S. B. A. W., Wien, Jahrg. 1868, 173, op. 1, 179, 252, 274, 287; isti, Weitere Mitth. über die Mundart von Gottschee, ib., 1870, 451, 465, 4
2 CONC 70, 488; Levec, LZ 1885, 121–3; isti, ib. 1886, 315; Ilešič, ZMS 1905, 88; Prijatelj, ib., 1907, 236; isti, IMK 1909, 20–1, 79–80; Mole, Carn. 1910, 106; Prijatelj, ČZN 1910, 157–60, 172–3; Kidrič; ib. 375, 379; isti, Paberki o Ko
2 CONC rytku LZ 1910; isti, Carn. III (1912), 28–43; Polec, Kraljestvo Ilirija 224, 225, 242, 243; Kidrič, Dobrovský, kazalo; isti, Prešeren II, 88, 336, 374; Kočevski zbornik 1939, 47, 133, 134, 179, 180, 181, 182, 251, 252, 253, 254, 2
2 CONC 55, 323, 324; Kidrič, Korytkova smrt in ostalina 1947, 36; isti, PA 280; M. Uršič, Kopitarjeva pisma Jožefu R-u, Razprave SAZU, Razred za filol. in lit. vede, II (1956), 117–44. Slika: Langusov portret v Nar. galeriji v Lj. (repro
2 CONC dukcija v SPor 1951, št. 286 kot Anton Jožef R.). U. M.
2 CONT
2 CONT Milena Uršič Ursic, Milena: Rudez, Joseph (1793-1846) . Slovenian biography. Slovenian Academy of Sciences and Arts, SAZU Science and Research Center, 2013. http://www.slovenska-biografija.si/oseba/sbi524204/#slovenski-biografski-
2 CONC leksikon (March 5, 2020) . Original publication in: Slovenian Biographical Lexicon: 9th volume . Raab - Schmid. Alfonz Gspan et al. Ljubljana, Slovenian Academy of Sciences and Arts, 1960.
